[64.786771, "o", "[approval] bash: cd /tmp && W=/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl; python3 \"$W/pip\" install --force-reinstall --no-index --no-cache-dir \"$W\" 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && W=/usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-p…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n  [tools] 12 call(s) without changing a file — nudging to decide\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 12 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp && pip3 --version 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"; python3 -m pip --version 2>&1; echo ===; python3 -c \"import pip; print('import pip ->', pip.__file__)\" 2>&1 — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && pip3 --version 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"; python3 -m pip --versi…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 13 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Restored. Now verifying an actual install works end to end, into a temp t…\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp && rm -rf /tmp/pipcheck && mkdir -p /tmp/pipcheck && timeout 120 pip3 insta"]
[64.786798, "o", "ll --no-cache-dir --target /tmp/pipcheck six 2>&1 | tail -5; echo \"exit=${PIPESTATUS:-$?}\" — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && rm -rf /tmp/pipcheck && mkdir -p /tmp/pipcheck && timeout 1…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 14 tool call(s) so far\r\n[approval] bash: cd /tmp && python3 -c \"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/tmp/pipcheck'); import six; print('six', six.__version__)\" 2>&1; rm -rf /tmp/pipcheck; echo cleaned — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash cd /tmp && python3 -c \"import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/tmp/pipcheck');…\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\nFixed. `pip` works again.\r\n\r\n**What was wrong:** the `pip` package directory itself was missing from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/` — only the orphaned `pip-24.3.1.dist-info` metadata was left behind. So `/usr/local/bin/pip3` existed and ran, but died on `from pip._internal.cli.main import main` with `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'pip'`. Nothing was wrong with your PATH, proxies, or pip"]
[64.786986, "o", " config (there is no pip.conf anywhere); the script was fine, the module it imports was gone.\r\n\r\n**Why the obvious fixes didn't work:** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` bails out with \"Requirement already satisfied: pip in ... (24.3.1)\" — it trusts the stale metadata and never restores the files. It's worth knowing, because it looks like it succeeded.\r\n\r\n**The fix I applied:** installed the bundled wheel directly, forcing the reinstall and skipping the metadata check:\r\n\r\n```\r\npython3 /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl/pip \\\r\n  install --force-reinstall --no-index --no-cache-dir \\\r\n  /usr/local/lib/python3.13/ensurepip/_bundled/pip-24.3.1-py3-none-any.whl\r\n```\r\n\r\nThat runs pip's zipimport entry point from inside the wheel, so it works without a working pip, and needs no network.\r\n\r\n**Verified:** `pip3 --version` and `python3 -m pip --version` both report 24.3.1 from `/usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pip`; `import pip` resolves; and a real install end to end (`pip3"]
[64.787016, "o", " install --no-cache-dir --target /tmp/pipcheck six`) succeeded and the installed package imported. The temp target is cleaned up; nothing was left behind in the repo.\r\n\r\nTwo things I noticed but did not change: your `/usr/bin/python3` (3.11.2, the Debian one) has no pip either — that's normal for Debian, which ships it as a separate `python3-pip` package, so `pip3 install` there fails independently of this bug. And an empty `.gnomon-audit/` log plus `.gnomon/` were already in this directory; not mine to touch.\r\n\r\n[result] role=implementor model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=15 surface=fb32316fd0e2\r\n"]
